    "issued_at": "2026-10-20T11:42:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "d421f381-4c02-4585-b2b2-1f9526b8caa0",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 201142\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n442 AM PDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* An incoming trough brings widespread rain and mountain snow \n  chances, gusty winds, and cooling temperatures this week. \n  \n* Increasing confidence for a dry conditions and seasonable \n  temperatures this weekend. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Monday.\n\nOur next system begins to encroach on the region today in the form \nof a closed low spinning off the CA coast. Over the remainder of \nthe work week, it will slowly move through the forecast area, \neventually transitioning to an open wave as it phases with a PacNW\nshortwave. Northward moisture advection and upper-level \ndivergence in the left exit region of a jet streak will spur \nisolated to scattered showers and storm today. Best chances \n(25-50%) are generally northwest of I- 15 and in Mohave County. \nThe slow-moving nature of the system results in a similar setup on\nWednesday. Precipitation chances are higher across the board \n(+25%) on Wednesday due to additional moisture and colder air \naloft, but the same places will be favored. Finally, the main vort\nmax associated with the low swings through on Thursday, bringing \nthe best potential for widespread precipitation (60-90% in most \nlocations). However, the phasing with the PacNW shortwave sends a \nsecondary vort max down the backside and keeps the trough overhead\nthrough the end of the work week. As a result, isolated to \nscattered shower chances linger on Friday. \n\nOverall, best odds of precipitation impacts are in the southern \nGreat Basin, Inyo County, and northern Mohave County where chances \nof 1\"+ range from 25-50%. Thunderstorm development will aid this \npotential, but training convection is going to be required given \nthe flow aloft. The biggest \"bust\" potential is in the southern \nMojave Desert (think of the area bounded between Twentynine Palms,\nBarstow, Las Vegas, Kingman, and Lake Havasu), where models have \nthis zone kind of caught in a dry slot between the two main \nprecipitation regimes. This area has a 40-70% chance of 0.10\" or \nless. There is a minor wintry component of this system as snow \nlevels bottom out around 8kft in the Sierra. However, as they do, \nprecipitation will largely be done, so any impacts are expected to\nbe confined to elevations above 9kft where forecast snow totals \nrange from 3-10\" (the higher amounts near the crest). Lastly, \ngusty south-southwest winds (20-40 mph) will accompany this system\nas well, but any impacts should be confined to the local lakes.\n\nBy Saturday, ensemble guidance has our area firmly on the backside \nof the trough. We will see a dry northerly flow that quickly \nscours out moisture and precipitation chances, and produces some \ngusty north winds. On Sunday and Monday, the flow weakens and \nbecomes more zonal/southwesterly, keeping us dry with light winds \nand slightly below-normal temperatures.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds\nwill continue following typical daily directional patterns with\nsome variability, remaining light except during this afternoon\nwhen southeasterly winds increase to around 8KT. FEW-SCT clouds \naround 10kft are expected through the period, with isolated \nafternoon and early evening convection over the higher terrain \nthat is not expected to impact the terminal. VFR conditions will \ncontinue.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Across the region,\nwinds will continue following typical daily directional patterns,\nwith speeds around 5-10KT. One exception to this is across the \nwestern Mojave Desert/KDAG where easterly winds this afternoon are\nexpected to increase to 12-15KT with gusts to 20-25KT through \nsunset. Additionally, KBIH in the northern Owens Valley will \nexperience gusty up-valley southeasterly winds late morning into \ntonight, with gusts peaking at around 25-30KT this afternoon \nthrough late evening. Isolated to scattered thunderstorms are \nexpected across the region this afternoon and evening, especially \nover higher terrain. Have included VCTS mention for KBIH this \nevening for thunderstorms expected to develop over surrounding \nmountains, however, confidence is too low at this time to include \nTS elsewhere. VFR conditions prevail outside of thunderstorm \nactivity with ceilings expected to remain at or above 10kft.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
